    "ix.0": "Grade 7 · Texas History · §113.19 · Problem-Based Learning",
    "ix.1": "Spindletop, 1901 — When Oil Changed Everything",
    "ix.2": "A Problem-Based Learning unit. Students step into a Texas town during the 1901 oil boom, take on a stakeholder’s point of view, and work a real, ill-structured question — building from surface to deep to transfer learning. The teacher is a guide, not the answer key.",
    "ix.3": "<b>Driving question:</b> January 1901. The Lucas Gusher at Spindletop, near Beaumont, has struck oil — more than anyone has ever seen. Overnight, thousands pour in; land prices explode; water and order are strained. Our town must decide how to handle the boom so it helps the community and lasts. What should the community do?",
    "ix.4": "The three-phase path (do them in order)",
    "ix.5": "Problem-solving is a <b>transfer</b> move — it only works once students have knowledge to reason with. So the problem in Phase 3 is deliberately <b>gated</b> behind Phases 1 and 2.",
    "ix.6": "Build the knowledge",
    "ix.7": "Vocabulary, key facts about the oil boom, a jigsaw read, and map work with Texas regions. ~1–2 periods.",
    "ix.8": "Connect & organize",
    "ix.9": "Cause–effect concept map, primary sources, points of view, and a structured argument. ~1–2 periods.",
    "ix.10": "Solve the problem",
    "ix.11": "Meet the problem, take a role, investigate, propose & defend a solution, debrief. ~2–4 periods.",
    "ix.12": "⛔ <b>Gate:</b> don’t open the Phase 3 problem until students have finished the surface and deep activities. Meeting the problem too early turns inquiry into guessing.",
    "ix.13": "Big idea & objectives",
    "ix.14": "<b>Big idea:</b> The Spindletop oil boom transformed Texas from an <b>agrarian</b> economy of cotton and cattle into an <b>industrial</b> one. Rapid change created winners, losers, and hard choices about land, resources, growth, and fairness.",
    "ix.15": "<b>Know:</b> what Texas’s economy was like before oil, how the Lucas Gusher changed it, and how the boom pulled people, money, and industry to the state.",
    "ix.16": "<b>Understand:</b> the same boom looked different from each stakeholder’s point of view — and rapid growth brings costs as well as gains.",
    "ix.17": "<b>Do:</b> run the problem-solving process end to end — define a problem, gather information, weigh options, choose and defend a solution, and evaluate it (§113.19(c)(23)(B)).",
    "ix.18": "Standards this unit is aligned to (§113.19)",
